      },
      "rev_n_4_876bc9_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_876bc9_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_876bc9",
        "thought_text": "Consider using a greedy approach to partition the sequence. Start by identifying potential candidates for the strictly increasing sequence by iterating through the sorted sequence and ensuring each subsequent element is greater than the previous one. The remaining elements would form the strictly decreasing sequence.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "3443469695789977764",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794391030.9836302
      },
      "rev_n_5_4847a5_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_4847a5_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_4847a5",
        "thought_text": "Implement a two-pointer technique on the sorted sequence. One pointer starts from the beginning (for the increasing sequence) and moves forward while ensuring each element is greater than the last. The other pointer starts from the end (for the decreasing sequence) and moves backward while ensuring each element is less than the last. This method helps in efficiently partitioning the sequence.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "3443469695789977764",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794391030.983642
      },
      "rev_n_6_b434b5_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_b434b5_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_b434b5",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the frequency of each element in the sequence. If any element appears more than twice, it's impossible to partition the sequence into strictly increasing and decreasing parts. Otherwise, use the unique elements to construct the increasing and decreasing sequences by sorting and partitioning based on their natural order.",
